---
export const title = 'Variants';
export const order = 200;
import HighlighterCard from '../../../../components/HighlighterCard.astro';
import DemoComponentCard from '../../../../components/DemoComponentCard.astro';
---

<p>Here are several predefined button styles, each serving its own semantic purpose.</p>

<style is:inline>
  .button-demo-title {
    margin-block-end: 1rem;
  }


  .button-demo-area {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 1rem;
    height: 100%;
  }
</style>

<DemoComponentCard lang={'html'} inline>

<div class='button-demo-container'>
  <div class='text-title button-demo-title'>Default Variants</div>
  <div class='button-demo-area'>
    <wc-button variant='elevated'>Elevated</wc-button>
    <wc-button>Filled</wc-button>
    <wc-button variant='tonal'>Tonal</wc-button>
    <wc-button variant='outlined'>Outlined</wc-button>
    <wc-button variant='text'>Text</wc-button>
    <wc-button variant='neo'>Neo-Brutalism</wc-button>
  </div>
</div>



  <div class='button-demo-container'>
   <div class='text-title button-demo-title'>Toggle Variants - Unselected</div>
   <div class='button-demo-area'>
   <wc-button variant='elevated' toggle>Elevated</wc-button>
   <wc-button toggle>Filled</wc-button>
   <wc-button variant='tonal' toggle>Tonal</wc-button>
   <wc-button variant='outlined' toggle>Outlined</wc-button>
   </div>
  </div>

  <div class='button-demo-container'>
   <div class='text-title button-demo-title'>Toggle Variants - Selected</div>
   <div class='button-demo-area'>
    <wc-button variant='elevated' toggle selected>Elevated</wc-button>
    <wc-button toggle selected>Filled</wc-button>
    <wc-button variant='tonal' toggle selected>Tonal</wc-button>
    <wc-button variant='outlined' toggle selected>Outlined</wc-button>
   </div>
  </div>

</div>

</DemoComponentCard>


<DemoComponentCard lang={'html'}>
  <wc-button block>Block</wc-button>
</DemoComponentCard>

<br/>

<style is:inline>
  strong {
    font-weight: bold;
  }
</style>

<p>The <code>level</code> property serves as <strong>syntactic sugar</strong>, allowing you to quickly define a button's visual identity without manually configuring individual style attributes.</p>
<p>Each level resolves to a color and a variant. The same table applies to <code>wc-icon-button</code> and <code>wc-link-button</code>.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Color</th><th>Variant</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>primary</code></td><td><code>primary</code></td><td><code>filled</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>secondary</code></td><td><code>surface</code></td><td><code>tonal</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>tertiary</code></td><td><code>primary</code></td><td><code>text</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>danger</code></td><td><code>danger</code></td><td><code>filled</code></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>The level is resolved at render time, so changing it later takes effect. A <code>color</code> or <code>variant</code> you set wins over it, one property at a time: <code>level="danger" variant="outlined"</code> is an outlined danger button. The <code>color</code> and <code>variant</code> properties read back the resolved values.</p>

<DemoComponentCard lang={'html'} inline>
  <wc-button level='primary'>Primary</wc-button>
  <wc-button level='secondary'>Secondary</wc-button>
  <wc-button level='tertiary'>Tertiary</wc-button>
  <wc-button level='danger'>Danger</wc-button>
</DemoComponentCard>
